ode[above=0.45cm of input4, labelnode] {(d) Bypass 3: Persistent Recurrence}; \end{tikzpicture} \caption{The parity barrier and three structural bypasses. (a) Fixed-scale quantised inference (FSQI) with fixed latency cannot compute global parity across arbitrarily large boards. Here fixed latency means a bounded number of sequential computation steps, independent of board size. (b) Two frames expose $\Delta$ for restoration when the predecessor has nim-sum zero. (c) Distinct policy heads recover complementary information through complete rollouts. (d) Persistent recurrence accumulates the nim-sum row by row and retains summaries across positions. Each update has constant depth; the full scan has depth growing with $N$.} \label{fig:barrier-blueprint} \end{figure}
